## P0: Core Rendering Abstraction

**Status**: Not Started
**Effort**: Medium (10-15 hours)
**Dependencies**: None
**Spec Reference**: User requirements for incremental migration
**Status Reference**: STATUS-libgdx-migration.md (Current architecture section)

### Description
Create the fundamental abstraction layer that decouples rendering from business logic. This is the foundation that allows us to swap JOGL/Processing renderer for LibGDX renderer without touching animation code.

### Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Create `IRenderer` interface with all methods needed by OnScreen.java
- [ ] Create `IMouseHandler` and `IKeyHandler` interfaces for event abstraction
- [ ] Create `RenderContext` class to hold renderer instance (singleton pattern)
- [ ] Interface covers ALL rendering operations currently in OnScreen.java:
  - Background clear
  - 3D primitives (point, line, box)
  - Transformation stack (translate, rotate, pushMatrix, popMatrix)
  - Projection (ortho, screenX, screenY)
  - 2D overlay (text for FPS)
  - Mouse/keyboard events
- [ ] Documentation: Javadoc for each interface method explaining behavior
- [ ] Unit tests: Mock implementation of IRenderer for testing

### Technical Notes
- Keep interface minimal - only what OnScreen.java actually uses
- Don't try to abstract away all of Processing, just the rendering
- Use float[] for matrices if needed for applyMatrix() equivalent
- Consider separate interface for batch rendering later (optimization)

### Implementation Hints
```java
// Example pattern for RenderContext singleton
public class RenderContext {
    private static IRenderer instance;

    public static void setRenderer(IRenderer renderer) {
        instance = renderer;
    }

    public static IRenderer getRenderer() {
        return instance;
    }
}
```

