## P3: Performance Benchmarking and Optimization

**Status**: Not Started
**Effort**: Medium (8-12 hours)
**Dependencies**: P1 LibGDX Renderer Foundation
**Spec Reference**: User requirements for performance
**Status Reference**: STATUS-libgdx-migration.md (Performance risks section)

### Description
Measure and optimize rendering performance to ensure LibGDX renderer matches or exceeds Processing renderer, especially for point cloud rendering with 1000+ LEDs.

### Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Benchmark Processing renderer (baseline)
- [ ] Benchmark LibGDX renderer (initial)
- [ ] Profile CPU usage with VisualVM or similar
- [ ] Profile GPU usage if possible
- [ ] Identify performance bottlenecks
- [ ] Optimize renderer (batching, culling, etc.)
- [ ] Achieve 30fps sustained with full node cloud
- [ ] Memory usage comparable or lower than Processing
- [ ] Document performance characteristics
- [ ] Create performance regression tests

### Technical Notes
**Metrics to Measure**:
- Frame rate (target: 30fps locked)
- Frame time (target: <33ms per frame)
- CPU usage (target: <50% on target hardware)
- Memory usage (baseline vs. LibGDX)
- GC pauses (should be minimal)
- Render calls per frame

**Performance Profiling**:
```java
// Frame timing
long frameStart = System.nanoTime();
render();
long frameTime = System.nanoTime() - frameStart;
System.out.println("Frame time: " + (frameTime / 1_000_000) + "ms");
```

**Optimization Strategies**:
1. **Batch Rendering**: Collect all points, render in one draw call
   ```java
   // Instead of: shapeRenderer.begin/end for each point
   // Do: Collect all points, render once
   shapeRenderer.begin(ShapeType.Point);
   for (Node node : nodes) {
       shapeRenderer.setColor(node.r/255f, node.g/255f, node.b/255f, 1f);
       shapeRenderer.point(node.x, node.y, node.z);
   }
   shapeRenderer.end();
   ```

2. **Frustum Culling**: Don't render points outside camera view
   ```java
   if (camera.frustum.pointInFrustum(x, y, z)) {
       shapeRenderer.point(x, y, z);
   }
   ```

3. **Custom Mesh**: For maximum performance, build point cloud as single mesh
   ```java
   Mesh mesh = new Mesh(false, numPoints, 0,
       new VertexAttribute(Usage.Position, 3, "a_position"),
       new VertexAttribute(Usage.ColorPacked, 4, "a_color"));
   mesh.setVertices(vertices);
   mesh.render(shader, GL20.GL_POINTS);
   ```

4. **Shader Optimization**: Custom point shader with configurable size

**Target Hardware**:
- macOS ARM64 (Apple Silicon) - primary
- Raspberry Pi 4 (headless) - secondary
- May need different optimization strategies per platform

---

## Testing Strategy

### Unit Tests
- IRenderer interface implementations (mock, processing, libgdx, headless)
- Math utilities (compare against Processing results)
- Color utilities (verify ARGB encoding, HSB conversion)
- Noise utilities (compare against Processing noise)
- Coordinate projection (screenX/screenY accuracy)

### Integration Tests
- Full application lifecycle (create → render → dispose)
- Renderer switching (can swap renderers at runtime?)
- Input handling (mouse/keyboard events)
- LED output correctness (UDP data matches expected values)

### Visual Tests
- Screenshot comparison (Processing vs. LibGDX)
- Side-by-side rendering (dual window mode for comparison)
- LED mapping verification (physical lights display correctly)

### Performance Tests
- Frame rate benchmarks (sustained 30fps)
- Memory leak tests (run for hours)
- CPU/GPU profiling

